{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790277660,"nonce":"n2LytLF3+xmLS6xNGqZCjp5Hh6M=","title":"Python: a caller cannot widen the typed list a per-signature instance returns","message":"A closed item called with lists of different kinds gets an instance per signature (80749056), and each instance returns its slice or copy at the argument's kind. A caller that then writes another kind into the result raises TypeError, where the item's own function, reached with an Object parameter, returned a List\u003cAny\u003e that takes any write:\n\n    def mid(xs):\n        return xs[1:3]\n\n    ss = mid([1, 2, 3, 4])\n    ss.extend([\"p\", 2.5])     # CPython: [2, 3, 'p', 2.5]; here: TypeError\n    ts = mid([\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"])\n    ts.insert(0, 7)\n    us = mid([(1, 2), (3, 4), (5, 6)])\n    us[1] = \"q\"\n\ndea6d08b prints CPython's output, because xs joins to Object and the slice is dynamic. The same program with only the int call fails on dea6d08b as well: this is 202606d (a typed list does not widen when a write puts another kind in it, including through a return value), which instances now reach from call sites that used to be dynamic.\n\nPinned by conformance/list/instance_result_writes.py in KNOWN_FAILURES. The fix is 202606d's unification: the caller's writes join into the element kind the instance's result is created with.","files":null}]}